# Expectation record for 05_pointers/008_array_decay_parameters.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 008_array_decay_parameters
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Array-to-pointer parameter adjustment for one-, two- and three-dimensional arrays
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every parameter walk stays inside the array actually passed, and each element count is
supplied by the caller from the array's own size rather than assumed.  The flattened index
is computed from pointer-to-row arithmetic within one array object multiplied by the row
width, never by subtracting element pointers from two different rows, and its row and
column arguments are bounds-checked before use.  sizeof a decayed parameter is never
printed, so the output does not depend on pointer width.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
